<h4 id="5-mach_absolute_time">5. <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">mach_absolute_time()</code></h4>

<p>There happens to be such a value on our iPhones — the CPU’s clock cycles (ticks). This <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">tick</code> value can be used to describe time, and <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">mach_absolute_time()</code> returns the number of <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">ticks</code> the CPU has been running. This <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">tick</code> count can be converted into seconds or nanoseconds through certain transformations, linking it directly to time. However, this <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">tick</code> count restarts from zero after every device reboot, and it also stops counting when the iPhone locks its screen and goes to sleep.</p>

<p><strong>Note: <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">mach_absolute_time()</code> is not affected by the system time; it’s only affected by device reboots and sleep behavior.</strong></p>

